WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.880
In this video, let's look
at RStudio's pro tool set

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.360
and how you can use RStudio
Workbench to create new

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.087
JupyterLab sessions.

00:00:15.087 --> 00:00:17.170
You also have the choice
of using Jupyter Notebook

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.560
or VS Code with Python, but in
this case, let's start there.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.570
Well, then publish
to RStudio Connect

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:25.510
so stakeholders can see and
view and easily understand

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.150
that data.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.812
So let's open this new
JupyterLab session.

00:00:30.812 --> 00:00:32.770
And the beautiful thing
about RStudio Workbench

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.360
is that you can
authenticate users,

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.970
you can have the resources that
are needed on this platform

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:41.680
and share the various resources
between your collaborators.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.680
So I have this example that
I've already put together.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.460
And so in this case,
we have all this data

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.320
from the Center for
Near-Earth Objects

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.350
or asteroids and
comets from NASA.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.300
And so I've got a
lot of columns here,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.780
way more than I want to
work with, about 40 or so.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.260
But what I want to do
is just get to about 15

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.379
of those columns and
understand the data a bit better.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:08.890
So I've put together this
JupyterLab Notebook,

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:11.290
and in this case, you
can see that we've

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.720
pulled in some Python
visualization libraries

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.040
that we're going to use, like
Pandas, Seaborn, Plotly Express.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.650
And we can use those to take
a closer look at the data.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.810
So here we have a column
list that we've just

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.090
pulled in that
specific data that we

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.000
would like from the
CSV file using Pandas,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.590
we're reading that CSV.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.570
And then we have our
column list above which

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.950
we're pulling in here.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.780
So to make it a little
easier to understand

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.460
the data I have
renamed my columns,

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.140
you can, you know,
of course, just use

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.730
the columns that come existing.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:49.170
But in this case, we're
displaying the first five rows

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.850
to get a quick view of that.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.970
And I've also broken my data
down by asteroids and comets

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.440
based on the orbit class,
which helps you to do that.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.530
So we've broken
that data up a bit

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.460
with Pandas. Now using Seaborn

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.333
you could start to do things
like visualize that data.

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:07.000
There are some things that we're
doing to observe the data here.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.550
In this case, you know,
we're using Plotly

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.419
to have a more
interactive visualization.

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:14.640
We can see the majority
of these comets

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.090
or actually, yeah,
comets in particular

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:21.295
have been discovered in
the last two decades.

00:02:21.295 --> 00:02:22.920
And then going down,
there's other ways

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.210
we could look at the data.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.150
But the point here is that
we've done a few things

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.872
to plot our data.

00:02:29.872 --> 00:02:31.330
And then we can,
you know, describe

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:35.650
that data some and maybe even
have some things that we embed

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:37.150
in this document,
like in this case,

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:39.880
we've got the ability
to embed, you know,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:43.702
HTML and more visualize some
things from NASA and so on.

00:02:43.702 --> 00:02:45.160
But you get the
basic point, you've

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.200
got a data glossary here.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.540
So let's take this
Jupyter Notebook now,

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.200
and we'll publish it
directly to RStudio Connect.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.880
So to do that,
you'll actually need

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.820
to open a new session
in RStudio Workbench.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:00.850
And so we have the option
to have a JupyterLab

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:04.850
session or a Jupyter Notebook
session to build our notebook.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:07.840
But to publish it we'll
actually go to Jupyter Notebook

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.320
to do this inside of
RStudio Workbench.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.440
And once we have this
session open then we'll see,

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.560
we have another option for
publishing to RStudio Connect.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.800
So we'll go to
that same notebook.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:25.300
And once this is open and
we'll give it a moment,

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.610
we'll have the option
to publish directly.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.850
So now we see we have
this option for publishing

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:33.760
to RStudio Connect.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.350
And the first time
that we do this

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:39.700
we'll actually have a dialog that
pops up and will have the need

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:45.760
to bring in our server
name, server address.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.210
So once we have that.

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.840
And we'll also want to go
back to RStudio Connect

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.350
and get an API key.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.630
OK, so once we've logged
into RStudio Connect,

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.930
we see in the top right corner
that we've got the user login

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.300
icon, let's select
that and then you

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.800
can select API keys to
set up this initial key.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.760
So we're just going to
set up a new key here,

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.360
and then once we have
that, we'll copy it.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.180
We can select that to
copy or just select here,

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:20.519
and we'll close this out and
go back to RStudio Workbench.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:24.240
We'll paste in that API key
from RStudio Connect, and just

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.100
give this a name as test server
and use the default secure

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.250
connection settings.

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:32.475
We'll add this server.

00:04:32.475 --> 00:04:34.350
Then when we publish
this, we have the option

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.392
to either publish the document
with the source code

00:04:36.392 --> 00:04:40.320
or publish the finished document
only as a static notebook.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.350
In this case, we want to publish
the document with the source

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:46.050
code, and then we'll
also select the files

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:47.940
that are associated with it.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.640
So we're going to actually
select the CSV file

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.000
and then there was an image
file for that notebook as well.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.850
OK, so now we're going to
publish using the existing

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:58.867
requirements.txt file.

00:04:58.867 --> 00:05:01.450
We could also generate one if
we needed to but in this case,

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:03.250
we already have one,
so we'll publish it.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.110
And we'll set this
as a new location.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:13.470
And publish.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:17.040
So let's take a look at this
"SpaceTracker" notebook that's

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.950
inside of RStudio Connect.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:24.540
So we have this loading here
with our table and our plots,

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.530
and everything is functional
from the HTML standpoint

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.692
within RStudio Connect.

00:05:30.692 --> 00:05:32.400
The great thing about
this is that now we

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.050
can share this actual set
of data and information

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:40.390
with end users in a way
that we can control.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:42.330
So we could have it
so that anyone could

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.410
login and view this document.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:47.500
We may have specific users
that we want to set up.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.670
So in this case, we'll
set up "Profound Publisher"

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.720
to have access.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:57.080
And then we can set, you
know, copy this URL and share

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:00.480
that with them to use anywhere.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.210
And another thing
that you can see here

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:05.300
is that we can schedule this
document, so in this case,

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.640
you could set a particular
time for that to run.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.780
In this case, I have a CSV file,
but you could have live data

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:13.700
that you've connected
to here, and this

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.520
could rerun that document
at a particular time

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.620
and even send that off
to them in an email

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:20.040
and publish it to them.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:24.980
So this is really helpful to
control that the information is

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:27.500
current. Now the end
user has full access

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:30.590
and can easily take a look
at this notebook anywhere

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:34.850
and be able to see the
particular data live

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:37.840
and totally up to date.

